titre$KEY TO THE GENERA DESCRIBED IN THIS BOOK To identify a taxon it is necessa 
com$adapt GF juillet 2015

1,0$  Source///A Guide to Temperate Myxomycetes 1989/// An English translation by A. Feest and Y. Burggraaf of De Nederlandse Myxomyeeten by N.E. Nannenga-Bremekamp
1,0$ remarque préalable : To identify a taxon it is necessary to have complete, fully ripe and dry fructifications. The colour may be completely different when the specimen is moist: for instance a white lime scale may appear grey or brown.
1,0$illustrations : *Figure numbers refer to numbers in the key$$f"Myxomycetes_Nannega_1989.jpg"
1,0$

1,0$Spores borne on thin stalks on the outside of the sporophore. Sporophore very watery in fresh condition, with many pillars that may be branched; white or very pale beige, yellow or pink$Ceratiomyxa.
1,2$Spores not on stalks, but free in a fructification; the peridium may be fugacious
1,0$ cf fig 1

2,3$No capillitium or pseudocapillitium present inside the fruit body (beware of contamination by fungal threads which are recognizable by their cellular structure), peridium never white from lime deposition; rarely (e.g. sometimes in Perichaena) with rhomboid crystals on the peridium
2,11$Capillitium and/or pseudocapillitium present as tubes, threads, perforated membranes or fraying membranes etc.
1,0$
2,11$Capillitium and/or pseudocapillitium present as tubes, threads, perforated membranes or fraying membranes etc. : if containing globular lime see 53 =>


3,0$Fruit body a very small, white, pink, colourless or pale beige, stalked sporangium (most frequently noticed in moist chamber cultures)$Echinostelium
3,4$Fruit bodies larger; or if small, dark or coloured
4,0$Fruit body a stalked sporangium with a locally thickened wall, these thickened parts may form a cup below and usually a reticulum above, or persist as a reticulum only; the resistant parts, and sometimes also the spores, containing lime globules (in the literature referred to as "dictydine granules")$Cribraria.
4,5$No peridial reticulum
4,0$ cf fig 4

5,6$Fructification a pseudoaethalium, consisting of appressed sessile sporangia; vertical walls seemingly connected. Spore mass rust-brown
5,7$Fructification in the form of scattered sporangia and/or plasmodiocarps
6,0$Component sporangia more-or-Less isodiametric, often not only appressed but superimposed. Peridium containing lime granules, sometimes with persistent reticulate thickenings. Plasmodium brown or black$Lindbladia. cf fig 6
6,0$Component sporangia 2-3 times taller than wide; no dictydine granules. Plasmodium bright pink or vermilion$Tubifera. cf fig 6*


*Figure numbers refer to numbers in the key
7,8$Fructifications up to twice as long as wide; spherical, kidney-shaped, sometimes angular or flattened.
7,9$Fruit bodies more than twice as long as wide, vermiform, sometimes branched, or oblong and then vertically compressed . cf fig 7*

8,0$Spore mass bright yellow. Sporangia 05-10 mm in diameter$Perichaena.
8,10$Spore mass of a different colour, if yellow then the sporangia are smaller
9,0$Spores free$Licea.
9,0$Spores in clusters$Enteridium Liceoides.cf fig 9*

10,0$Peridium single, containing lime globules, sometimes with reticulate thickenings$Lindbladia.
10,0$Peridium without lime globules, consisting of rarely one to usually two or three appressed layers, sometimes papillate on the inside, often with absorbed particles outside, sometimes with preformed angular plates which may have a knobbly margin or lid. Usually very small$Licea*.
10,0,n$'The conidia of some ascomycetes are very similar to Licea (some have even been described as such e.g. Licea hungarica and L. didermoides [ the latter proved to be Orbicula parietina)). A few fungal hyphae may be found at the base of the sporangia which can be recognised by their cellular construction. The sporangia released from Pilobolus are also similar to Licea especially as the capsule wall is acellular, so great care is necessary when collecting myxomycetes near dung.


11,12$Capillitial tubes wide, Mostly more than 2 urn in diameter, in optical section the wall is usually recognizable; often yellow, pink or red, hollow (when submerged directly in water, air is sometimes trapped inside); with structure (surface of tubes not smooth), without lime. Spore mass rust-coloured, yellow, red, pink, brown olive or ochraceous, rarely grey (never lilac-grey, purple-grey or black)
11,20$Capillitium or pseudocapillitium tubes not visibly hollow, usually thin, often dark with lilac shades or red-brown, sometimes colourless; smooth or with ornamentations. Spore mass white, nearly colourless, pinkish beige, ochraceous, rust-coloured, brown or with purple or lilac shades to black
11,20,n$The conidia of some ascomycetes are very similar to Licea (some have even been described as such e.g. Licea hungarica and L. didermoides [ the latter proved to be Orbicula parietina)). A few fungal hyphae may be found at the base of the sporangia which can be recognised by their cellular construction. The sporangia released from Pilobolus are also similar to Licea especially as the capsule wall is acellular, so great care is necessary when collecting myxomycetes near dung. 
11,0$ cf fig 11

12,13$Capillitial tubes show spiral structure
12,17$Capillitial tubes not showing spiral structure or only very weakly so in part of the tubes; decorated with spines, warts, half-rings, clubs, or ridges which sometimes form a fragmented reticulum
13,14$Tubes almost unbranched, often rather short, with many free ends, lying loose inside the sporangium= "elaters" (if connected to the sporangium on both sides ref. Prototricha metallica)
13,15$Tubes long and unbranched, or branched and often united into a net, with few free ends
14,0$Tubes ending in sharp points, the spiral structure prominent$Trichia.
14,0$Tubes with blunt or rounded tips; spiral structure weak$Oligonema and Calonema. cf fig 14*

15,0$Peridium of two or three layers, thick, sometimes rather brittle, dark, often in part black. Spore and capillitial mass red-brown or rusty red. Capillitium not birefringent .$Metatrichia.
15,16$Peridium single or double, rarely triple, never (in part) black. Spores and capillitial mass never red-brown or rusty. Capillitium birefringent
15,0$ cf fig 15

16,0$Capillitial tubes with distinct spiral structure, usually connected to the peridium at one end, free ending at the other (some species of Arcyria have distinct spirals on the capillitium which is not however birefringent$Hemitrichia. 
16,0$Capillitium tubes with very vague, irregular spirals, sometimes with coarse rings round them, usually attached to the peridium at one end, the other free, blunt$Oligonema and Calonema.
17,0$Fructification an aethalium, 3-30 mm in diameter, wall usually rather thick, usually consisting of several layers. Spores usually delicately reticulate. Pseudocapillitium tubular$Lycogala.
17,18$Fructification in the form of sporangia and/or plasmodiocarps, less than 25 mm in diameter when undehisced (compare also small aethalia of Lycogala conicum and Lycoga/a sp.)
17,0$ cf fig 17

18,0$Sporangia heaped, angular from mutual pressure, almost isodiametric, pale salmon­pink, later beige (plasmodium white or pale pink)$Arcyodes.
18,19$Not as above (not Sporangia heaped, angular from mutual pressure, almost isodiametric, pale salmon­pink, later beige )
18,0$ cf fig 18

19,0$Peridium single, basal part of the peridium persistent as a cup or funnel; single, membranous. Usually stalked or on a small foot. Capillitium forming an usually elastic net, ornamented with rings, half-rings, spines, warts and/or finer ridges that may locally form a reticulum$Arcyria. cf fig 19
19,0$Peridium double, the basal part of the peridium often sessile on a wide base which may remain as a cup, often with a regular rim. Capillitium covered with spines or small warts, often with constrictions (like the segments of a caterpillar), often sparse, branched, not or only sparsely anastomosing; spore mass usually yellow$Perichaena. cf fig 19*


20,0$Sporangia very small, always stalked; white, pale beige or pink (not very easy to see in nature, but found regularly in moist chamber cultures of bark and dead wood). Capillitium, if present, rather rigid$Echinostelium.
20,21$Fructifications larger, darker, different
20,0$ cf fig 20

21,0$Capillitial threads or tubules long, thin, coiled. Peridium thin, translucent, opalescent. Sporangia pink when fresh, later beige. Spore mass concolourous$Calomyxa.
21,22$Not as above  (not Capillitial threads or tubules long, thin, coiled. Peridium thin, translucent, opalescent. Sporangia pink when fresh, later beige. Spore mass concolourous )
21,0$ cf fig 21

22,0$Fructifications in the form of sessile sporangia and plasmodiocarps. Capillitium attached to the base and to the peridium, rather straight, more or less radiating. Spore mass beige, pink, ochraceous or rust-coloured. Peridium with an opalescent, iridescent or metallic lustre$Dianema
22,0$Fructifications small, about 1 mm in diameter, aethalia. Pseudocapillitium sparse and arched, not radiating $Enteridium lobatum
22,23$Not as above (not Fructifications small, about 1 mm in diameter, aethalia. Pseudocapillitium sparse and arched, not radiating  )
22,0$ cf fig 22

23,24$Fructifications, aethalia or pseudoaethalia. Spore mass brown, rust-coloured, beige, yellow or ochraceous. True capillitium pale and sparse or usually absent. Pseudocapillitium present or, rarely, absent. No lime present
23,26$Fructifications either aethalia or pseudoaethalia or sporangia and/or plasmodiocarps. Spore mass dark brown to black, rarely rust-coloured, lilac or lilac­grey. Capillitium usually present and abundant, horny, red-brown, dark purple-brown to black, of thread-like tubules or almost colourless to very pale yellow or lilac. Pseudocapillitium inside the aethalia or pseudoaethalia sometimes present. with or without lime encrustations
24,0$Aethalia (or pseudoaethalia) very strongly depressed. Wall tessellate on top, Pseudocapillitial strands T -shaped, passing down perpendicularly from the corners of the tessellate peridial plates$Dictydiaethalium.
24,25$Not as above (not Aethalia (or pseudoaethalia) very strongly depressed. Wall tessellate on top, Pseudocapillitial strands T -shaped, passing down perpendicularly from the corners of the tessellate peridial plates )
24,0$ cf fig 24

25,0$Fructifications aethalia. Pseudocapillitium spongy, consisting of perforated plates or a three-dimensional arch system, or finely branched all over, or in the form of fraying plates$Enteridium.
25,0$Fructifications pseudoaethalia (Component sporangia appressed and with complete walls). Capillitium and pseudocapillitium sparse, consisting of fine threads and/or tubes$Tubifera.
25,0$ cf fig 25

26,27$Fructifications without lime encrustations
26,48$Fruit bodies with lime encrustations (on or in the peridium and/or capillitium and/or stalks and columella and/or hypothallus)
27,0$Fructifications sessile sporangia with a gelatinous outer layer when moist.$Colloderma.
27,28$No gelatinous outer layer around the fructification
27,0$ cf fig 27

28,29$Fructifications in the form of aethalia or pseudoaethalia
28,31$Fructifications in the form of sporangia and/or plasmodiocarps
29,0$Aethalia with septate vesicles attached to the capillitium. Spores 90-105 urn in diameter$Brefeldia.
29,30$No septate bladders attached to the capillitium
29,0$ cf fig 29

30,0$Aethalia with a fugacious wall. Capillitium either forming a net with axillary expansions, or pseudocapillitium arborescent with fraying branches. Spores large ([10­] 12-15 urn in diameter)$Amaurochaete.cf fig 30
30,0$Pseudoaethalia; the sessile sporangia fused basally and centrally; no common wall; capillitium, if present, reticulate, connected to a regular or irregular columella. Often with fragments of peridium remaining. Spores usually less than 12 urn in diameter (exception: S. c01ljlue1ls)$Symphytocarpus.cf fig 30*

31,32$Peridium membranous, thin, persistent, or only partly persistent viz. a collar around the stem, or as plates on or inside the capillitium (if the peridium is persistent, rather sturdy and the capillitium radiating and coloured, compare with limeless forms of Diderma; and if the capillitium is a colourless net compare with limeless forms of Physarum)
31,41$Peridium fugacious
31,0$ cf fig 31 b c d e 

32,0$Sporangia sessile or subsessile$Colloderma.
32,33$Sporangia stalked
33,34$The whole peridium shining and more-or-less persistent, dehiscing into large pieces
33,36$Peridium persisting only as a collar, or as scales attached to the capillitium or at the  base or apex of the columella
34,0$Columella reaching almost to the apex of the cylindrical sporangium, tapering  upwards$Stemonitopsis typhina.
34,35$Columella reaching to about half-way up the sporangium; ending bluntly or  merging into a few main branches of the capillitium
35,0$Columella ending bluntly, capillitium more-or-less radiating with many, roughly  equal branches arising mostly from the apex of the columella$Lamproderma.cf fig 35
35,0$Columella dividing into a few thick main branches which taper outwards.$Collaria. cf fig 35*

36,37$Peridium remaining as a cup or a small collar around the stem
36,39$Peridial plates or scales persistent.
37,0$Capillitial branches radiating from the apex of the columella, about the same thickness throughout (peridium usually rather persistent.$Lamproderma.
37,38$Not as above (not Capillitial branches radiating from the apex of the columella, about the same thickness throughout (peridium usually rather persistent )
38,0$Stalk hollow, rather evenly red-brown in transmitted light. Capillitium dichotomously branched, sparsely anastomosing$Macbrideola. cf fig 38
38,0$Stalk black, fibrous (see base in transmitted light). Capillitium branched and anastomosing$Collaria.cf fig 38*

39,0$Sporangia appressed, sessile or almost sessile. Remains of peridium loose or connected to the capillitium$Symphytocarpus.
39,40$Sporangia not appressed, stalked. Remains of the peridium connected to the capillitium
40,0$Peridial plate apical, often funnel-shaped, connected to the apex of the columella. Most of the capillitium emerges from the margin of this peridial plate$Enerthenema.
40,0$Peridial plates connected to the tips of the capillitial threads, the columella reaches up to half-way in the sporangium$Clastoderma.
41,0$Sporangia appressed, sessile or subsessile. The capillitium not forming a surface net. Columella hollow, horny, often rather flat and irregular$Symphytocarpus.
41,42$Sporangia distinctly stalked, usually free
42,60$Columella and stalk in transmitted light, horny, red-brown and hollow; (this can sometimes be seen only in the centre after the air in the stalks has disappeared). Sporangia cylindricaL
42,43$Columella and stalk fibrous, but often opaque and black in transmitted light; check the base of the stalk or columella after squashing. Sporangia spherical or short-to long-cylindrical
42,0$ cf fig 42 et 42'


43,44$Sporangia very small, spherical. Columella reaching to the centre of the sporangium, where it merges into the main branches of the capillitium
43,45$Sporangia spherical or cylindrical. Columella usually reaching nearly to the apex, and tapering. Capillitium joined along its whole length to the columella
44,0$Capillitial threads branching only$Paradiacheopsis.
44,0$Capillitial threads branching and anastomosing$Collaris elegans.
44,0$ cf fig 44

45,0$Primary branches of the capillitium more-or-Less horizontal, perpendicular to the columella; branches rather stiff, neither forming loops nor undulate, showing many free ends at the periphery$Comatricha (subgenus Laxaria).
45,46$Primary branches of the capillitium not strikingly horizontal; free ends few
45,0$ cf fig 45

46,0$Capillitial threads flexuose, undulate usually looped on the surface, not forming a surface net, often elastic and falling away from the stalk$Comatricha (subgenus Sinuaria).
46,47$Capillitium branched and anastomosis into an internal net not of strikingly undulating threads, often with a fragmented surface net especially in the basal part .. 47
46,0$ cf fig 46

47,0$Sporangia spherical or cylindrical. Stalk usually longer than the sporangium. Capillitium usually without expansions in the internal net..$Comatricha (subgenus Comatricha).cf fig 47
47,0$Sporangia cylindrical. Stalk shorter than the sporangium. Capillitium usually with prominent expansions in the internal net.$Stemonitopsis.cf fig 47*

48,49$Capillitium without lime, usually coloured lilac-brown)
48,53$Capillitium containing lime globules, usually colourless or pale yellow*.
48,53,n$'If the capillitium is lime less and colourless, it may be an almost lime less form of PhysarulIl or Badhamia
48,53,n$(Physaraceae).If the capillitium is lime less and colourless, it may be an almost lime less form of Physarum or Badhamia
49,0$Peridium without lime, often iridescent or with a metallic shine. Lime concentrated inside the stalk and the columella and often also inside the hypothallus$Diachea.
49,50$Peridium containing lime
49,0$ cf fig 49

50,51$Peridium with crystalline lime in the form of loose crystals or conjugated into scales or appressed crystalline plates, or a (sometimes smooth) lime-shell of very compact crystals.cf fig 50
50,0$Peridium with lime globules (exceptionally the lime globules are crystalline and then birefringent). The peridium usually consists of two layers (these may be so closely adherent that they appear to be single). The outer layer may be smooth, shell-like (subgenus Diderma); or the outer layer may be cartilaginous and usually dark red­brown or ochraceous-brown (subgenus Leangium)$Diderma.cf fig51

51,0$Fructifications in the form of aethalia, usually large and rather thick$Mucilago. 
51,52$Fructifications in the form of sporangia and! or plasmodiocarps
51,0$ cf fig 51

52,0$Fructifications in the form of stalked sporangia. Peridium covered with separate lime scales with a radiating crystal structure$Lepidoderma
52,0$Fructifications in the form of sessile or stalked sporangia and/or sessile plasmodiocarps. Peridium with lime crystals, appressed into a crust or stellate or compressed into scales$Didydium.
52,0$ cf fig 52


53,0$Fructifications somewhat resembling grape pips, brown, shining sporangia, on weak hypothallus-like stalks, or rarely sessile. Capillitial tubes duplex: in part, filled with lime globules, in part limeless. Peridium thick, brittle$Leocarpus.
53,54$Not as above (not Fructifications somewhat resembling grape pips, brown, shining sporangia, on weak hypothallus-like stalks, or rarely sessile. Capillitial tubes duplex: in part, filled with lime globules, in part limeless. Peridium thick, brittle )
54,0$Capillitium a pillar-or arch-system of tubes completely filled with lime globules. Fruit bodies in the form of sporangia and/or plasmodiocarps$Badhamia. cf fig 54
54,55$Capillitium a net with local expansions (lime nodes) filled with lime globules. Fruit bodies in the form of sporangia and/or plasmodiocarps or aethalia. cf fig 54*

55,0$Fructifications in the form of vermiform branched plasmodiocarps, transverse striped and with orange or red spots above (the stripes correspond with calcareous cross-walls)$Willkommlangea.
55,56$Not as above ( not Fructifications in the form of vermiform branched plasmodiocarps, transverse striped and with orange or red spots above (the stripes correspond with calcareous cross-walls)  )
55,0$ cf fig 55

56,0$Fruit bodies in the form of goblet-shaped sporangia, dehiscing with a lid; the lid is not always distinct, but the goblet is rather persistent. Peridium (sub) cartilaginous. Usually there is a pseudocolumella$Craterium.
56,57$No distinct opercula, no persistent goblets
56,0$ cf fig 56

57,0$Fruit bodies in the form of aethalia. cf fig 57$Fuligo.
57,0$Fruit bodies in the form of sporangia and/or plasmodiocarps. cf fig 57*$Physarum.


60,0$Capillitial threads form an almost complete surface net supported by a three­dimensional internal net. Sporangia cylindrical$Stemonitis.
60,0$Capillitium lax, branched dichotomously with free ends perpendicular to the columella. Sporangia black, very long and thin, flexuose$Stemonaria longa.
